[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JVM Flags, JIT Compilation, Tiered Compilation, and Inlining in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how the Java Virtual Machine executes bytecode is crucial for high-performance enterprise applications. This tutorial explores the internals of Just-In-Time (JIT) compilation, tiered compilation, method inlining, and the JVM flags used to tune them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Role of JIT Compilation"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When Java source code is compiled, it becomes bytecode. At runtime, the JVM interprets this bytecode. To improve performance, the JIT compiler compiles frequently executed bytecode (hot spots) into native machine code directly."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Tiered Compilation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern JVMs use Tiered Compilation (enabled by default via -XX:+TieredCompilation) to balance startup time and peak performance. It operates in multiple levels:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Level 0: Interpreter (no profiling)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Level 1: Simple C1 compiler (full optimization without profiling)."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Level 2: Limited C1 compiler (with basic profiling)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Level 3: Full C1 compiler (with full profiling)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Level 4: C2 compiler (Graal in some advanced distributions), performing aggressive optimizations."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Method Inlining"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method inlining is one of the most powerful optimization techniques performed by the JIT compiler. It replaces a method call directly with the body of the target method, eliminating the overhead of stack frame creation and jumping."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

public int compute() {
    // After inlining, the call to add() is replaced by (a + b)
    return add(5, 10);
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Essential JVM Flags for Tuning JIT and Inlining"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can configure JVM compilation behavior using specific command-line flags. Here are the most important ones:"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-XX:+TieredCompilation: Enables the tiered compilation framework."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-XX:MaxInlineLevel=N: Sets the maximum depth of nested inlining (default is usually 9)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-XX:MaxInlineSize=N: Sets the maximum bytecode size of a method to be inlined (default is 35 bytes)."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-XX:+PrintCompilation: Prints messages to the console whenever a method is compiled by the JIT."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Example: Analyzing JIT Compilation"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"To observe JIT activity in your application, run your Java application with diagnostic flags enabled."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"java -XX:+PrintCompilation -XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+PrintInlining -jar my-application.jar"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering these flags and understanding how the JIT compiler inlines methods under tiered compilation, you can significantly improve the performance and throughput of your Java applications."}]
